1
00:00:06,308 --> 00:00:08,810
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

2
00:00:08,970 --> 00:00:10,752
[SPEAKER_04]: You cannot see remaining alive.

3
00:00:11,612 --> 00:00:18,378
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the body, the material aspect, the fleshy aspect, the rūpa, gets deformed, destroyed.

4
00:00:18,859 --> 00:00:21,821
[SPEAKER_04]: They cannot remain in any consciousness in regard to it.

5
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:34,872
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's how you recognize that then your eye, your nose, your ears are that ultimate away from you, that you just have obviously been appropriating for so long, and it's so close that you can't see it.

6
00:00:35,964 --> 00:00:39,606
[SPEAKER_04]: So, then, you just assume it's in you, it's yours, and you are the result.

7
00:00:39,966 --> 00:00:42,887
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's kind of the expression of you, so to speak.

8
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:51,411
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you start to see that, when you think about... people think about the eye, it is the certain expectation that they will find the real eye somewhere else.

9
00:00:53,452 --> 00:00:53,932
[SPEAKER_04]: But you won't.

10
00:00:54,432 --> 00:00:57,733
[SPEAKER_04]: Thoughts you have about the eye are all you can have about the eye.

11
00:00:58,334 --> 00:01:02,215
[SPEAKER_04]: Because eye itself, it's always going to be ultimate, negative.

12
00:01:02,556 --> 00:01:03,276
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't appear.

13
00:01:04,641 --> 00:01:11,587
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what appears is that because of which there is sort of appearance and that's determined by that I.

14
00:01:12,508 --> 00:01:19,754
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even you think I, you have to stop regarding that as a real I or as a non-real I or as a both real or not real.

15
00:01:19,774 --> 00:01:24,838
[SPEAKER_04]: You can just recognize I can only have thoughts that stay, that stand in place of an I.

16
00:01:25,238 --> 00:01:30,663
[SPEAKER_04]: And when I say an I, that's another thought that stands in the place of an I, which is another thought, which is another thought.

17
00:01:31,023 --> 00:01:32,765
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can never access the I.

18
00:01:33,974 --> 00:01:36,657
[SPEAKER_04]: You can only be a second, sort of, to it.

19
00:01:37,558 --> 00:01:41,682
[SPEAKER_04]: Whatever thought, whatever abstraction, whatever is going to be secondary to it.

20
00:01:42,003 --> 00:01:49,831
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's all it's necessary to recognize that, because then you see, oh, by being inaccessible, you cannot even conceive ownership of it.

21
00:01:50,312 --> 00:01:52,074
[SPEAKER_04]: And by that, can you even access thoughts?

22
00:01:53,135 --> 00:01:53,976
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, exactly.

23
00:01:54,136 --> 00:01:54,877
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, if you start

24
00:01:55,752 --> 00:01:57,412
[SPEAKER_04]: Refining that principle further...

25
00:01:58,013 --> 00:02:00,113
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, it's relatively refined when it comes to body.

26
00:02:00,733 --> 00:02:04,454
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the same principle when it comes to mind, but it's much harder to see, as the Buddha said himself.

27
00:02:05,515 --> 00:02:07,635
[SPEAKER_04]: Because putuṣa can get rid of the body...

28
00:02:07,655 --> 00:02:09,816
[SPEAKER_04]: So, do you think...

29
00:02:09,996 --> 00:02:17,418
[SPEAKER_04]: If you extend the same principle, then you realize that thoughts themselves, in their nature, arise on their own.

30
00:02:18,618 --> 00:02:24,762
[SPEAKER_04]: You, you sort of manage them, you intend upon them, but then you realize that that intention is secondary to the arising of thoughts.

31
00:02:24,862 --> 00:02:33,167
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, intention shares the same nature of cannot be owned, because it's founded upon things that are inaccessible to ownership.

32
00:02:34,008 --> 00:02:38,751
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how the mind gets free from the subtlest concealings, and becomes an arahant.

33
00:02:39,671 --> 00:02:48,377
[SPEAKER_04]: So then you can think, and will, and still not own it, while you're willing, and thinking, and deciding, and telling off, and whatever else arahants do.

34
00:02:48,767 --> 00:02:49,968
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, normally, in a way.

35
00:02:52,169 --> 00:02:55,650
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the same principle, but it's much harder to see it on the level of the mind.

36
00:02:56,991 --> 00:03:03,094
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you see it on the level of the body, thoroughly, then, yes, it's far from impossible.

37
00:03:05,435 --> 00:03:06,596
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's the whole point.

38
00:03:06,696 --> 00:03:08,517
[SPEAKER_04]: It's you spilling over.

39
00:03:08,597 --> 00:03:12,279
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same sense that Atavada spills over the five aggregates and owns them.

40
00:03:13,268 --> 00:03:22,413
[SPEAKER_04]: Once that's gone, well, then you still have perceptions in regard to the sense bases that spill over, that assume the access to the eye, to the nose, hence the sensuality.

41
00:03:23,634 --> 00:03:31,178
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the only benefit of sensual sights, that benefit can only pertain to the fleshy eye.

42
00:03:31,658 --> 00:03:33,880
[SPEAKER_04]: Nothing else can benefit from sight.

43
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:38,642
[SPEAKER_04]: Skin and the sense of touch can only benefit from touching things.

44
00:03:38,702 --> 00:03:39,703
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't access that.

45
00:03:40,455 --> 00:03:47,802
[SPEAKER_04]: But by fully assuming access to the body, to the eyes, you are then drawn by that which these things want.

46
00:03:48,222 --> 00:03:52,486
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, you think you are accessing all the external sensuality.

47
00:03:52,526 --> 00:03:53,868
[SPEAKER_04]: You are touching pleasant objects.

48
00:03:53,888 --> 00:03:55,209
[SPEAKER_04]: You are tasting pleasant sounds.

49
00:03:56,150 --> 00:03:57,071
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry, pleasant tastes.

50
00:03:57,751 --> 00:03:59,233
[SPEAKER_04]: And you are hearing agreeable sounds.

51
00:03:59,333 --> 00:03:59,513
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

52
00:03:59,733 --> 00:04:00,934
[SPEAKER_04]: Ear is hearing the sounds.

53
00:04:00,994 --> 00:04:01,795
[SPEAKER_04]: Cannot be different.

54
00:04:03,096 --> 00:04:04,518
[SPEAKER_04]: Eye is seeing the sights.

55
00:04:05,751 --> 00:04:08,893
[SPEAKER_04]: But you, when you say I, you assume the I.

56
00:04:09,394 --> 00:04:11,075
[SPEAKER_04]: Hence, you are pulled by the sights.

57
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:29,568
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why the mindfulness, this mindfulness that I'm talking about, peripheral or letting it endure, is necessary, because you'll never see a connection between your thoughts about the I, your sights, and that

58
00:04:31,467 --> 00:04:40,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Indirect, fleshy organ that is simultaneously present, because of which all of this is possible, that in return defines that which is an I.

59
00:04:40,810 --> 00:04:44,471
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why the sense base is defined by the sense object.

60
00:04:44,531 --> 00:04:48,833
[SPEAKER_04]: In itself, you can't even conceive the extent of it.

61
00:04:51,133 --> 00:04:55,835
[SPEAKER_04]: But if the mindfulness is developed in the way we were talking yesterday, you will be able to hold a thought

62
00:04:56,833 --> 00:05:10,560
[SPEAKER_04]: of the present organ of an eye, because of which I'm thinking of an eye and doing all of this without lapsing, without either jumping too much onto the external notion of what eye is, or thinking of it, you know, eye is this, eye is in me, I am, basically.

63
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:16,162
[SPEAKER_04]: You're able to see both ends without, kind of, sort of, snapping towards one.

64
00:05:16,422 --> 00:05:16,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

65
00:05:16,823 --> 00:05:21,885
[SPEAKER_04]: If the mindfulness is developed to the extent necessary, basically.

66
00:05:22,555 --> 00:05:25,237
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you know, like a rubber band, sort of, you stretch, you stretch.

67
00:05:25,257 --> 00:05:28,800
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's either going to sort of snap from this end or from this end.

68
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,781
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't be able to kind of include both ends.

69
00:05:32,442 --> 00:05:41,529
[SPEAKER_04]: And both ends are included through development, through discernment of enduring, endurance of the simultaneous theories and two things, right?

70
00:05:41,549 --> 00:05:42,970
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it could be simultaneously present.

71
00:05:43,070 --> 00:05:45,212
[SPEAKER_03]: For that, you basically need mindfulness.

72
00:05:45,232 --> 00:05:46,933
[SPEAKER_03]: Without mindfulness, you cannot do anything.

73
00:05:47,013 --> 00:05:47,514
[SPEAKER_03]: Impossible.

74
00:05:47,534 --> 00:05:48,294
[SPEAKER_03]: Just impossible.

75
00:05:49,095 --> 00:05:49,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Impossible.

76
00:05:51,076 --> 00:05:51,897
[SPEAKER_04]: With mindfulness,

77
00:05:52,705 --> 00:05:56,547
[SPEAKER_04]: It does not necessarily bring knowledge, unless you know what you're supposed to know.

78
00:05:57,028 --> 00:06:00,630
[SPEAKER_04]: But certainly, without it, there is absolutely no basis for any discernment.

79
00:06:01,391 --> 00:06:05,093
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you already have discernment, then all you need is the practice of mindfulness.

80
00:06:05,173 --> 00:06:08,315
[SPEAKER_04]: And satipaṭṭhāna is enough for somebody with the right view.

81
00:06:08,535 --> 00:06:12,558
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the only way that will lead to full purification of the being and anything else.

82
00:06:13,518 --> 00:06:14,359
[SPEAKER_04]: As it will describe.

83
00:06:14,499 --> 00:06:18,001
[SPEAKER_04]: Because what else would lead to the full purification?

84
00:06:18,661 --> 00:06:22,584
[SPEAKER_04]: Then just sticking to that clear vision and developing it to the extent necessary.

85
00:06:29,087 --> 00:06:33,328
[SPEAKER_01]: What I've thought about from time to time is the assumption of that something...

86
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:38,369
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's in the context of what Ananda says, the I is better than the world, which one is deeper than the other.

87
00:06:38,389 --> 00:06:45,451
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always the assumption that, you know, there's visions, but there's also something behind that point of view.

88
00:06:45,491 --> 00:06:51,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you assume that spatiality with the assumption that there's a spatial kind of being.

89
00:06:52,092 --> 00:06:54,873
[SPEAKER_01]: I am here and things are elsewhere.

90
00:06:55,033 --> 00:06:56,553
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can't stop that.

91
00:06:57,623 --> 00:07:12,847
[SPEAKER_04]: But when you do have such thoughts, you start including them in that proper mindfulness of knowing that all of that is possible, because there is the actual operating flesh of an I, that you also know at the time that that's your thought in regard to it.

92
00:07:13,647 --> 00:07:18,769
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's an accurate thought, because it doesn't conceive it anymore as that hidden entity in the hidden.

93
00:07:37,958 --> 00:07:38,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

94
00:07:38,299 --> 00:07:38,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

95
00:07:38,699 --> 00:07:44,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when they think of there, they think in some terms of, like, a further yonder.

96
00:07:44,023 --> 00:07:45,545
[SPEAKER_01]: They think on that level.

97
00:08:07,359 --> 00:08:10,582
[SPEAKER_04]: Instead of seeing that both are possible because there is already there.

98
00:08:11,863 --> 00:08:13,705
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what... that's the basis of phenomena.

99
00:08:13,785 --> 00:08:28,999
[SPEAKER_04]: That there... Nope.

100
00:08:29,019 --> 00:08:31,101
[SPEAKER_01]: I do think it's so often misconceived.

101
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:52,329
[SPEAKER_02]: The mindfulness of breathing, watching, you know, watching breaths, you can... you know that the body's there.

102
00:08:53,510 --> 00:08:56,091
[SPEAKER_02]: This is... this breath is happening.

103
00:08:56,151 --> 00:08:56,491
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

104
00:08:56,571 --> 00:09:00,974
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why the breathing is tāyāsandhāra, it's bound with body.

105
00:09:01,579 --> 00:09:03,460
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just inconceivable, one without the other.

106
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:06,681
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't feel.

107
00:09:07,702 --> 00:09:09,183
[SPEAKER_04]: The body is there when the breathing is there.

108
00:09:09,203 --> 00:09:10,443
[SPEAKER_04]: The breathing is there when the body is there.

109
00:09:11,064 --> 00:09:12,284
[SPEAKER_04]: But they're not the same thing.

110
00:09:12,924 --> 00:09:13,145
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

111
00:09:17,186 --> 00:09:18,527
[SPEAKER_04]: And then that body...

112
00:09:19,107 --> 00:09:20,148
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're just looking at one aspect of the body...

113
00:09:20,168 --> 00:09:30,533
[SPEAKER_04]: While the breathing is there, then the body can be manifested as the body, as the feeling, as the thoughts, as any other thing within the principle of two simultaneously present things while you're breathing.

114
00:09:31,367 --> 00:09:35,930
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can focus on both the aspects of bereavement.

115
00:09:37,891 --> 00:09:39,992
[SPEAKER_04]: But still not see the actual relationship.

116
00:09:40,573 --> 00:09:45,036
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, but you can't keep focusing on that breath, but you've got to see what relationship you have.

117
00:09:45,076 --> 00:09:45,256
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

118
00:09:45,756 --> 00:09:50,019
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why it's... Yeah, it's completely unnecessary to be focusing on anything.

119
00:09:50,679 --> 00:09:50,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

120
00:09:50,959 --> 00:09:51,940
[SPEAKER_04]: It actually takes you away.

121
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:52,440
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

122
00:09:53,141 --> 00:09:55,722
[SPEAKER_02]: But just, you can use the breath as one of those things.

123
00:09:55,742 --> 00:09:56,163
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just...

124
00:09:57,317 --> 00:09:58,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Steady is steady.

125
00:09:58,317 --> 00:09:59,998
[SPEAKER_02]: That's their vision, their mind.

126
00:10:00,118 --> 00:10:04,958
[SPEAKER_04]: The intention to breathe in the mind is in the actual, all actual breathing in regard to the body.

127
00:10:05,019 --> 00:10:12,720
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you look at their intention to breathe slowly, their intention is there, but this particular breath goes slowly.

128
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:15,581
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's beyond mind control.

129
00:10:16,101 --> 00:10:22,962
[SPEAKER_02]: All this, this pleasurable, well, experience of breath,

130
00:10:25,423 --> 00:10:34,433
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you start including your volition.

131
00:10:36,635 --> 00:10:38,998
[SPEAKER_04]: And it doesn't start introducing ignorance.

132
00:10:39,018 --> 00:10:42,081
[SPEAKER_04]: The volition usually comes from the sense of self.

133
00:10:42,101 --> 00:10:42,722
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you feel.

134
00:10:42,762 --> 00:10:44,964
[SPEAKER_04]: So, whenever you will, you engage in the sense of self.

135
00:10:46,583 --> 00:10:49,925
[SPEAKER_02]: But I choose to breathe slowly, because that's already there.

136
00:10:50,085 --> 00:10:50,485
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

137
00:10:50,505 --> 00:10:53,547
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a given possibility, based on the simultaneity of the present act of breathing.

138
00:10:54,468 --> 00:10:54,968
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you...

139
00:10:55,648 --> 00:10:59,791
[SPEAKER_04]: I choose to, and then you realize that I choose to, is already because of that.

140
00:11:00,471 --> 00:11:02,972
[SPEAKER_04]: So, then you stop adhering to it, as mine.

141
00:11:10,817 --> 00:11:16,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Description I always run into when you say, like, there's control and a lack of control.

142
00:11:20,943 --> 00:11:39,243
[SPEAKER_04]: When you fully get rid of the slightest assumption of control, outside of the lack of control, to a tiny degree or something, then you fully stop the assumption of any external entity that controls it.

143
00:11:52,885 --> 00:11:58,327
[SPEAKER_03]: But it seems that the agents say that the will is not necessary for this sense of self.

144
00:11:58,687 --> 00:12:05,230
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you can be completely paralyzed and still consider your body to be your body.

145
00:12:05,530 --> 00:12:06,530
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, the will of the mind.

146
00:12:07,251 --> 00:12:09,271
[SPEAKER_04]: That's ultimately mind, speech, body.

147
00:12:10,352 --> 00:12:11,572
[SPEAKER_04]: That's how the will is manifest.

148
00:12:11,592 --> 00:12:13,393
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why the action is three times the action.

149
00:12:14,890 --> 00:12:24,493
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, you can be paralyzed, but you can still be very much willing to bring your mind perpetuating the same type of mistake all to itself.

150
00:12:24,833 --> 00:12:31,915
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure, it's as this stops being usable, because it's gone, but it's still more necessarily... That's why a paralyzed person does not automatically become an Arhat.

151
00:12:32,135 --> 00:12:32,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

152
00:12:33,215 --> 00:12:35,816
[SPEAKER_04]: Because he remains ignorant just on a different level now.

153
00:12:35,916 --> 00:12:38,437
[SPEAKER_04]: He cannot express his ignorance in a bodily way on the path.

154
00:12:40,538 --> 00:12:41,538
[SPEAKER_04]: But if he can still speak,

155
00:12:42,246 --> 00:12:50,829
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if, in theory, say, you lose everything, it won't result in nibbāna.

156
00:12:51,010 --> 00:12:54,831
[SPEAKER_04]: It will result in the rebirth of another being affected with a vision.

157
00:12:56,452 --> 00:12:58,132
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you need to do that while you're alive.

158
00:12:58,873 --> 00:13:07,136
[SPEAKER_04]: Get the mind out of that self-centredness, so to speak, that sense of I am, and then the rebirth cannot affect you.

159
00:13:08,448 --> 00:13:11,209
[SPEAKER_02]: In this suicide, you can't actually kill the body.

160
00:13:11,229 --> 00:13:11,329
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

161
00:13:11,349 --> 00:13:14,869
[SPEAKER_04]: So, for another hand, like, for example, he can't kill himself.

162
00:13:15,849 --> 00:13:16,289
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

163
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:21,270
[SPEAKER_04]: Only acts can sort of prevent body from further, sort of, existing.

164
00:13:21,290 --> 00:13:22,311
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not a suicide.

165
00:13:22,731 --> 00:13:26,611
[SPEAKER_04]: Suicide is when the self-centered person, well, attempts to kill himself.

166
00:13:26,651 --> 00:13:28,332
[SPEAKER_04]: And kill himself by killing the body.

167
00:13:29,652 --> 00:13:29,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

168
00:13:30,252 --> 00:13:31,672
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you can't kill yourself directly.

169
00:13:31,692 --> 00:13:35,213
[SPEAKER_04]: You can just kill your body in a real second, kill your body, and then you wait for death to come.

170
00:13:35,653 --> 00:13:36,173
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, yes.

171
00:13:40,036 --> 00:13:45,201
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, the death of the body comes from a complete direction which you don't have access to.

172
00:13:45,842 --> 00:13:46,903
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, yeah, yeah, sure.

173
00:13:47,223 --> 00:13:48,665
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you don't even... yeah, exactly.

174
00:13:48,685 --> 00:13:53,470
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why you kill the body, hoping that it will result in death, because that's what you've seen happens.

175
00:13:53,970 --> 00:13:58,895
[SPEAKER_04]: But when death does come, it doesn't come from the way of you kill the body, it comes from the way of the mind.

176
00:13:59,015 --> 00:13:59,836
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what death is.

177
00:14:01,097 --> 00:14:02,779
[SPEAKER_04]: It comes from way behind, so to speak.

178
00:14:03,582 --> 00:14:05,483
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why putusas are always swept with the death.

179
00:14:05,503 --> 00:14:06,784
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, always swept with the flood.

180
00:14:07,304 --> 00:14:07,624
[SPEAKER_04]: Swept.

181
00:14:07,664 --> 00:14:10,146
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, it just comes from behind and takes you where you don't even see it.

182
00:14:11,167 --> 00:14:26,276
[SPEAKER_04]: But for an ārahant, who has developed that direction of death, that direction of not adhering to anything, seeing impermanence and everything, basically seeing that nothing is his, when things do get swept away, he's not there to be swept with it.

183
00:14:27,336 --> 00:14:31,959
[SPEAKER_04]: And he is absolutely inconceivable to fear death, because it's not death for him at all.

184
00:14:59,748 --> 00:15:20,541
[SPEAKER_04]: That's another assumption that, you know, when you see somebody, I don't know, a shot in the head, and you think, ah, they're dead.

185
00:15:21,481 --> 00:15:24,203
[SPEAKER_04]: Their body is gone, in terms of its functionality.

186
00:15:24,843 --> 00:15:27,585
